<div class="bt">Likutay Halachos — Orach Chaim, Volume 2</div>
<div class="ht">Hilchos Krias Shma She'al HaMitah<br>[Laws of the Bedtime <i>Shma</i>]<br>Halachos 1–2</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                    HALACHAH 1                                 -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn" style="font-size:1.25rem;text-align:center;">Halachah 1</div>

<div class="p" style="text-align:center;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Based on <span class="lr">LM I:36</span> (<i>"Bikrov Alai M'rayim"</i>) — see there the entire Torah.</div>

<div class="p">The general principle: every soul of Israel is rooted in the seventy souls of the House of Yaakov. And they are rooted in the seventy facets of Torah etc. And <span class="v">"corresponding to this"</span> etc. are the seventy languages etc. And know that the desire for licentiousness is the generality of the evil traits of the seventy languages etc. And for this reason our Sages of blessed memory expounded: <span class="v">"and you shall not go astray... and after your eyes"</span> — this refers to the desire for licentiousness etc., because it is the generality of the evil traits of the seventy languages etc.</div>

<div class="p">And know that the tikun for licentious thoughts that come upon a person is to say <span class="v">"Shma"</span> and <span class="v">"Baruch Shaim"</span> etc. For the desire for licentiousness comes from the turbidity of the blood etc. — which is <span class="h">Malchus</span> of the <span class="h">Sitra Achra</span>. And <span class="h">Malchusa d'Shamaya</span> [the Sovereignty of Heaven] is in the aspect of <span class="v">"the mistress"</span> — the G-d-fearing woman — which is in the aspect of the Sea of Shlomo standing upon twelve oxen, the twelve tribes of Y-ah. And when a person accepts upon himself the sovereignty of Heaven through these verses — including his soul among the twelve tribes of Y-ah and separating his soul from the souls of the <span class="h">Airev Rav</span> [Mixed Multitude] who derive from the harlot etc., which is the evil maidservant — then it is in the aspect of <span class="v">"I am fleeing from the presence of my mistress Sarai"</span> etc.: the desire flees and separates from him etc.</div>

<div class="p">For in these two verses — <span class="v">"Shma"</span> and <span class="v">"Baruch Shaim"</span> — there are twelve words, corresponding to the twelve tribes of Y-ah. And forty-nine letters, corresponding to the forty-nine letters in the names of the tribes of Y-ah etc. And through the acceptance of the sovereignty of Heaven etc., one's soul is included among the souls of the tribes of Y-ah — the aspect of the G-d-fearing woman. Then it is the aspect of closing the eyes during <span class="h">Krias Shma</span>, to indicate that through the acceptance of the sovereignty of Heaven, the <span class="mx">"beautiful maiden who has no eyes"</span> — which is the desire that encompasses the evil traits of the seventy languages etc. — [is subdued].</div>

<div class="p">And when the revelation of Torah comes, new souls are made from the revelation etc., each according to his aspect and grasp. For the Torah has two powers etc. And this is a great principle: from the Mouth of the Most High nothing goes forth but simple light, but according to the aspect of the vessel that receives the light, so the light is imaged within it etc. And this is the aspect of: <span class="v">"behold, I have received [the command] to bless, and He has blessed, and I cannot reverse it"</span> etc. And the essential tikun and perfection of the vessel is through guarding the <span class="h">Bris</span> [holy covenant]. And then one merits to receive the light and to image it in the aspect of blessing etc. — see there all of this well.</div>

<div class="p">It emerges that through <span class="h">Krias Shma</span>, licentious thoughts are subdued. For through <span class="h">Krias Shma</span> one is included in <span class="h">Malchus d'K'dushah</span>, among the twelve tribes of Y-ah etc. And this is the aspect of <span class="h">Krias Shma She'al HaMitah</span>. For the essential tikun of sleep is to be saved from evil thoughts. As we pray and beseech from Hashem in the <span class="h">berachah</span> of <span class="h">HaMapil</span> before sleep: <span class="v">"and may my thoughts and evil dreams and evil contemplations not frighten me"</span> etc. For it is known that the essential intensification of the flaw mentioned above — the flaw of the desire for licentiousness — is in the aspect of sleep, in the aspect of <span class="h">mikrei lailah</span> [nocturnal occurrences], G-d forbid. Therefore before sleep one must subdue these thoughts mentioned above. And for this reason our Sages of blessed memory ordained for us to recite <span class="h">Krias Shma She'al HaMitah</span> before sleep — which is a very great tikun for the flaw of the desire mentioned above, as explained in the Writings [of the Ari"zal]. For through reciting <span class="h">Krias Shma</span> and accepting upon oneself the sovereignty of Heaven, through this one separates his soul from the souls of the <span class="h">Airev Rav</span> etc. as mentioned above, and includes his soul among the twelve tribes of Y-ah etc. — which is the aspect of the G-d-fearing woman, the aspect of <span class="h">Malchus d'K'dushah</span>, the aspect of the mistress. Through this, the thoughts that derive from the harlot are subdued and flee from him, as mentioned above, in the aspect of <span class="v">"I am fleeing from the presence of my mistress Sarai."</span> As explained well in the Torah mentioned above — see there.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of what we say: <span class="v">"and may my bed be complete before You"</span> etc. A complete bed — this is the aspect of the bed of Yaakov, of the twelve tribes of Y-ah, which was a complete bed. As our Sages of blessed memory said on the verse <span class="v">"and Yisra'el bowed upon the head of the bed"</span>: that his bed was complete — twelve tribes of Y-ah without any refuse etc. And this is what we request before sleep: that our bed should be complete etc. — meaning as mentioned above: that we should be saved from evil thoughts etc. as mentioned above, that our soul should be included among the souls of the twelve tribes of Y-ah etc. — which is the aspect of a complete bed. And one who merits that his soul be included with them during sleep — his bed is also complete etc., as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="v">"and enlighten my eyes"</span> etc. For the essential flaw of the desire mentioned above is in the aspect of the eyes, as mentioned above — for it is the generality of the seventy nations mentioned above, which are the aspect of the <span class="h">shiv'in anpin chashuchin</span> [seventy dark facets] etc. as mentioned above. And when one merits that through sleep one's soul is included among the twelve tribes of Y-ah, as mentioned above — who are rooted in the seventy facets of Torah, which are the <span class="h">shiv'in anpin nehirin</span> [seventy luminous facets] — then one merits through sleep a new soul, a new revelation in Torah. For the soul is renewed during sleep within the <span class="h">emunah</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="h">Krias Shma</span>, in the aspect of <span class="v">"new each morning, great is Your faithfulness."</span> And one who merits can attain during sleep a new revelation in Torah. As we find with several <span class="h">tzadikim</span> who attained great attainments during sleep etc.</div>

<div class="p">And this is what we request before sleep: <span class="v">"and may my thoughts not frighten me... and evil contemplations, and may my bed be complete before You, and enlighten my eyes lest I sleep the sleep of death."</span> For it is all one — namely: to be saved from evil thoughts that derive from the harlot etc., as mentioned above; and to merit that one's soul be included among the twelve tribes of Y-ah through <span class="h">Krias Shma</span> — which is the aspect of a complete bed, as mentioned above. And then one merits the aspect of the <span class="h">shiv'in anpin nehirin</span> — the aspect of <span class="v">"enlighten my eyes"</span> etc. — the opposite of the seventy nations, which are the <span class="h">shiv'in anpin chashuchin</span>, the <span class="h">Sitra d'Mosa</span> [aspect of death]. Rather: to merit to separate one's soul from them during sleep through <span class="h">Krias Shma She'al HaMitah</span> — through which the seventy nations are subdued, meaning the desire mentioned above that encompasses them etc., as mentioned above. For through <span class="h">Krias Shma</span> one's soul is included among the twelve tribes of Y-ah, who are rooted in the <span class="h">shiv'in anpin nehirin</span> — the aspect of the seventy facets of Torah, the aspect of <span class="v">"enlighten my eyes"</span> etc., as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is what our Sages of blessed memory said: a <span class="h">talmid chacham</span> who is attached to the Torah constantly is exempt from <span class="h">Krias Shma She'al HaMitah</span>. For the essential purpose of <span class="h">Krias Shma She'al HaMitah</span> is to be saved from evil thoughts etc. and to merit the aspect of seventy facets of Torah, as mentioned above. Therefore a <span class="h">talmid chacham</span> who is attached to the Torah certainly does not need this. For he surely has no evil thoughts, since he is attached to the Torah constantly, and he is constantly connected to the aspect of seventy facets of Torah — for his thought is always holy, and he is guarded from the evil thought, from the <span class="mx">"thought that falsifies the Tree of Life."</span> Therefore he certainly merits automatically the revelation of Torah during sleep, and does not need <span class="h">Krias Shma She'al HaMitah</span> to subdue evil thoughts before sleep etc., as mentioned above. For he is attached to the Torah constantly and has no evil thought, for his thought is bound to the Torah constantly.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of confessing one's sins before sleep. For all sins and transgressions come from the seventy evil traits and desires of the seventy nations, which are the dark facets — the opposite of the seventy facets of Torah. Therefore before sleep one confesses one's sins and beseeches from Hashem atonement for them — in order to separate our souls from the souls of the <span class="h">Airev Rav</span>, from the seventy nations from which all sins derive, as mentioned above. In order that our souls should be able to ascend and be included among the souls of the twelve tribes of Y-ah, which are the aspect of the <span class="h">shiv'in anpin nehirin</span>, the aspect of the seventy facets of Torah etc., as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="v">"Vihi No'am"</span> that we say before sleep — the aspect of the Supernal Pleasantness, which is the aspect of the attainment of Torah, the aspect of the seventy facets of Torah. For this is the essential pleasantness, in the aspect of <span class="v">"her ways are ways of pleasantness."</span></div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of what we say: <span class="v">"behold his bed, that of Shlomo — sixty mighty men"</span> etc. And so we say then <span class="h">Birchas Kohanim</span>. For when one receives the light from above, it is simple light, and it is imaged for each person according to his aspect etc., as mentioned above. Therefore we say <span class="h">Birchas Kohanim</span> in order to image the light in the aspect of blessing. For the light that comes from above is closed and sealed — the aspect of <span class="v">"the <i>Kohen</i> shall scoop up."</span> Therefore the <span class="h">Kohanim</span> have the power to image that light — which is the aspect of <span class="v">"the <i>Kohen</i> shall scoop up"</span> — to image it in the aspect of blessing. Therefore they are commanded to bless Israel with <span class="h">Birchas Kohanim</span>, as explained in Hilchos Nesi'as Kapayim — see there. This is the aspect of <span class="h">Birchas Kohanim</span> said before sleep: to image the light in the aspect of blessing, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="v">"sixty mighty men"</span> etc. For our Sages of blessed memory expounded this verse regarding the Torah, as brought in Rashi's commentary there on this verse: <span class="v">"behold his bed, that of Shlomo"</span> — meaning the Ark where the Torah rests; <span class="v">"sixty mighty men around it"</span> — they are the six hundred thousand of Israel etc.; <span class="v">"trained in warfare, each man with his sword upon his thigh"</span> — they make mnemonic signs in the Torah so that it will not be forgotten from them, and they are teachers of the warfare of Torah etc. — see there in Rashi's commentary. Meaning as mentioned above: the Torah has two powers etc., and each person images the light according to his aspect. Therefore one must make mnemonic signs in the Torah so it will not be forgotten — meaning to make signs that are the aspect of vessels to receive the light in the aspect of goodness and blessing, so it will not be forgotten. Meaning: so that one will not forget the Torah and, G-d forbid, reverse the words of the living G-d and declare what is forbidden to be permitted etc. — which is the aspect of imaging the light in the opposite manner. Therefore one must be a teacher of warfare, and have one's sword upon one's thigh — which is the aspect of guarding the <span class="h">Bris</span>, as is known. And through this very thing one merits to make mnemonic signs in the Torah, which are the aspect of holy vessels to receive the light — in the aspect of full light, in the aspect of blessing — and to be saved from forgetfulness. Meaning: from reversing, G-d forbid, the words of the living G-d — from imaging the light in the opposite manner, G-d forbid.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of <span class="v">"sixty mighty men"</span> — the six hundred thousand of Israel who received the Torah. For the Torah was not given until Israel numbered six hundred thousand. For there are six hundred thousand letters in the Torah, corresponding to six hundred thousand souls of Israel — and each member of Israel has a letter in the Torah. Therefore it was impossible to receive the Torah until there were six hundred thousand of Israel. For each member of Israel, according to his grasp of the Torah, has the power to image the letters of the Torah according to his aspect. Therefore at the Standing at Har Sinai, when there were six hundred thousand of Israel, all holy in the holiness of the <span class="h">Bris</span>, as it is written: <span class="v">"the tribes of Y-ah"</span> etc. — <span class="mx">"My Name testifies about them"</span> etc. — therefore specifically then they merited the receiving of the Torah, for they had the power to image the entire Torah — which is comprised of their souls — for good and for blessing. Therefore they are called <span class="v">"sixty mighty men"</span> — for the imaging of light is the aspect of <span class="h">g'vurah</span> [might], which contracts and images the light in measure, for good and for blessing, as is known. Therefore we say this verse before sleep, in order to remember to image the light for good and for blessing. Amain.</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<!--                    HALACHAH 2                                 -->
<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="sn" style="font-size:1.25rem;text-align:center;">Halachah 2</div>

<div class="p" style="text-align:center;margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Based on <span class="lr">LM I:35</span> (<i>"Ashrei HaAm Zarka"</i>) — see there.</div>

<div class="p">The general principle: each person must guard his intellect from external wisdoms and from foreign thoughts, and all the more so must he guard his thought exceedingly from evil contemplations etc. For when a person introduces into his holy intellect external thoughts — which are external wisdoms — then the holiness of his intellect is diminished etc. And afterward, when a person guards himself from external wisdoms, not only that but he must also renew the intellect constantly etc. And the renewal of the intellect — which is the renewal of the soul — is through sleep, in the aspect of <span class="v">"new each morning, great is Your faithfulness"</span> etc. And during sleep the mind — meaning the soul — enters within <span class="h">emunah</span> [faith], in the aspect of <span class="v">"new each morning"</span> etc. And there are several aspects of sleep etc.</div>

<div class="p">But when a person places his intellect — meaning his soul — within the <span class="h">emunah</span> — meaning the plain sense of the Torah, meaning the conduct of business — in the aspect of <span class="v">"new each morning,"</span> in the aspect of sleep, then he must guard the <span class="h">emunah</span> so that the <span class="h">chitzonim</span> [external forces] do not draw sustenance from it, for it dwells among the <span class="h">chitzonim</span> etc. And one must draw the aspect of <span class="h">Chashmal</span> from the world of <span class="h">Binah</span> to clothe the <span class="h">Malchus</span> — meaning the <span class="h">emunah</span> — so that the idol-worshippers and the lands around him do not draw sustenance from it. And when a person conducts business with such faithfulness — like Rav Safra, who fulfilled <span class="v">"he speaks truth in his heart"</span> — this aspect becomes <span class="h">Chashmal</span>: a garment surrounding the <span class="h">emunah</span>. And then it shelters over its children so they do not draw sustenance from it etc. And this is the aspect of the offering of the <span class="h">Korban Tamid</span> and the burning of the <span class="h">Ketores</span> etc. — see there all of this well.</div>

<div class="p">And behold, according to this, the matter of <span class="h">Krias Shma She'al HaMitah</span> is very well understood. It is the faith of the Oneness that one must accept upon oneself before sleep. For sleep is the tikun of the mind, as mentioned above: the mind then enters within the aspect of <span class="h">emunah</span> and is renewed there, in the aspect of <span class="v">"new each morning, great is Your faithfulness"</span> etc. as mentioned above. Therefore before sleep one must say <span class="h">Krias Shma She'al HaMitah</span>, which is the essence of <span class="h">emunah</span> — so that through this one merits that one's mind, meaning one's soul, enters into the <span class="h">emunah</span> during sleep, in order to be renewed there in the aspect of <span class="v">"new each morning"</span> etc. as mentioned above. But when one places one's intellect — one's soul — within the <span class="h">emunah</span> during sleep, one must be careful to guard the <span class="h">emunah</span> itself, so that the <span class="h">chitzonim</span> do not draw sustenance from it, as mentioned above. Therefore one must draw the aspect of <span class="h">Chashmal</span> from the world of <span class="h">Binah</span> etc. as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of why one must recite <span class="h">Krias Shma</span> with self-sacrifice for the sanctification of the Name. For through self-sacrifice for the sanctification of the Name, one's soul is included in the aspect of <span class="h">Binah</span>, <span class="h">Ima Ila'ah</span> [the Supernal Mother], as explained in the Kavanos. And then one merits to draw from there — from the aspect of <span class="h">Binah</span> — the aspect of the <span class="h">Chashmal</span> mentioned above, in order to guard the <span class="h">emunah</span> during sleep, so that the <span class="h">chitzonim</span> do not draw sustenance from it, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is the aspect of the confessions that one must say in <span class="h">Krias Shma She'al HaMitah</span> before sleep. For the <span class="h">viduy</span> [confession] that one says is the aspect of <span class="h">emes</span> [truth], the aspect of <span class="v">"he speaks truth in his heart."</span> For the essential greatness of <span class="h">viduy</span> that is precious in the eyes of Hashem — when one confesses before Him — is the truth. Meaning: that one acknowledges the truth. This is the essential meaning of <span class="h">viduy</span>, from the root <span class="h">modeh</span> [acknowledges]. Meaning: one acknowledges and confesses that one sinned and transgressed against Him, and such and such one did. This is precious in the eyes of Hashem, since at the very least one recognizes one's sin and humbles oneself before Him, acknowledging and confessing the truth of what one did against Him.</div>

<div class="p">As it is written: <span class="v">"one who covers his transgressions will not succeed, but one who acknowledges and forsakes will receive mercy."</span> Meaning: one who is a liar and covers his transgressions will not succeed. But the opposite: when one is not a liar and does not cover one's transgressions in falsehood, but rather acknowledges and confesses the truth and recognizes one's sin and forsakes them — will receive mercy. And this is what Dovid said when he confessed his sin before Him, as it is written there: <span class="v">"against You alone have I sinned"</span> etc. He said there: <span class="v">"behold, You desire truth in the inner parts"</span> — regarding the <span class="h">viduy</span>, as Rashi explains there on this verse: <span class="v">"behold, You desire truth"</span> etc. — and behold I acknowledge the truth, for I have sinned.</div>

<div class="p">It emerges that the essential <span class="h">viduy</span> is the aspect of <span class="h">emes</span>. And this is the aspect of <span class="v">"he speaks truth in his heart."</span> For one must confess with all one's heart and accept upon oneself in truth, with all one's heart, not to do so again and not to return to folly. For the essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span> is in the heart, as it is written: <span class="v">"his heart shall understand and he shall return."</span> Therefore, through confessing one's sin before sleep — which is the aspect of <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, the aspect of truth in the heart, as mentioned above — which is the aspect of <span class="h">Binah</span>, where the essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span> and <span class="h">viduy</span> reside, as is known (this being the aspect of <span class="h">Yom Kippur</span>, as is known) — therefore through this one draws the aspect of <span class="h">Chashmal</span> from the world of <span class="h">Binah</span>, to guard the <span class="h">emunah</span> so that the <span class="h">chitzonim</span> do not prevail over him or draw sustenance from it. And then sleep is a great good for him — for through sleep one merits to renew one's mind and intellect, meaning one's soul, within the <span class="h">emunah</span>, in the aspect of <span class="v">"new each morning, great is Your faithfulness,"</span> as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is what is brought in the Writings of the Ari"zal: that <span class="h">Krias Shma She'al HaMitah</span> is a very great tikun for the severe transgression of wasteful emission, may the Merciful One save us. For the essential flaw of this transgression is a flaw of thought, as is known — through not guarding one's thought from contemplating this, one comes, G-d forbid, to this transgression, may the Merciful One save us. Therefore its essential tikun is through <span class="h">Krias Shma She'al HaMitah</span>, which is the essential tikun of the mind. For the essential tikun of the mind is through sleep, as mentioned above. For one must guard the mind very well from foreign thoughts, but even though one guards it well, nevertheless over time the mind becomes fatigued and one must renew it specifically through sleep — which is rest for the mind etc. as mentioned above. And then the essential tikun of the mind is through the <span class="h">emunah</span> that one merits during sleep — that one's mind enters into the aspect of <span class="h">emunah</span>, in the aspect of <span class="v">"new each morning"</span> etc. as mentioned above. But the <span class="h">emunah</span> itself needs to be guarded from the <span class="h">chitzonim</span> etc. as mentioned above. Therefore one must draw the aspect of <span class="h">Chashmal</span> etc. as mentioned above. And then, when one draws the aspect of <span class="h">Chashmal</span> to guard the <span class="h">emunah</span>, sleep is a great good for the mind — for the mind is renewed within the <span class="h">emunah</span> through sleep, as mentioned above. And one can guard one's mind well from external and foreign thoughts — which is the essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, the aspect of the <span class="mx">"<i>zarka</i> that is cast back to the place from which it was taken"</span> etc. as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And especially one who has already stumbled in his mind, G-d forbid — who introduced distress into the holiness of his intellect and contemplated evil contemplations until he came to this transgression, G-d forbid — then it is very difficult for him to guard his intellect. And even if he exerts himself with all his might to guard his intellect from foreign thoughts and contemplations, nevertheless his mind becomes fatigued each time, because his mind is habituated to evil contemplations and external thoughts. And now, when he comes to do <span class="h">teshuvah</span> and forces his mind with great exertion not to stray from the path, G-d forbid, and not to contemplate any foreign thought or contemplation, he requires great exertion and a great battle in his mind, because he is so habituated to evil thoughts. Therefore on account of this, his mind becomes exceedingly fatigued each time. Therefore he needs the aspect of sleep, to give rest to his mind. And through this his mind is repaired and renewed. And the essential tikun of sleep is through <span class="h">Krias Shma She'al HaMitah</span>, as mentioned above. Therefore <span class="h">Krias Shma She'al HaMitah</span> is the essential tikun of this transgression. For through <span class="h">Krias Shma She'al HaMitah</span> with intention and with self-sacrifice, one merits to repair and renew one's mind, as mentioned above. And this is one's essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span> and tikun — as explained above: the essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span> is guarding the mind etc., as mentioned above. And this one merits through the tikun of sleep, through <span class="h">Krias Shma She'al HaMitah</span>, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is: <span class="v">"behold, You desire truth in the inner parts."</span> Meaning: through the <span class="h">viduy</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="h">emes</span>, as mentioned above and as Rashi explains there — through this: <span class="v">"and in the hidden, You make wisdom known to me."</span> Through this one merits wisdom — meaning the aspect of the tikun of the mind. For through <span class="h">viduy</span> — the aspect of truth in the heart — one merits to renew one's wisdom and mind during sleep, as mentioned above. And this is <span class="v">"in the hidden"</span> — the aspect of sleep, which is the <span class="h">s'simu d'mochin</span> [closing of the mind]: the mind is closed and concealed then within the aspect of <span class="h">emunah</span>. And then specifically one merits new minds — the aspect of <span class="v">"wisdom You make known to me."</span> For then specifically, during sleep, the mind and wisdom are renewed, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p">And this is what we say before sleep: <span class="v">"into Your hand I entrust my spirit; You have redeemed me, Hashem, G-d of truth."</span> <span class="v">"Into Your hand I entrust my spirit"</span> — this is the aspect of our delivering our spirit and souls into the holy <span class="h">emunah</span>, which is the aspect of hands, the aspect of <span class="v">"his hands were <i>emunah</i>."</span> But we still fear that the <span class="h">chitzonim</span>, G-d forbid, might prevail to draw sustenance from the <span class="h">emunah</span> itself, as mentioned above. Regarding this we say: <span class="v">"You have redeemed me, Hashem, G-d of truth."</span> <span class="v">"Truth"</span> specifically — for through the aspect of <span class="h">emes</span>, one draws the aspect of <span class="h">Chashmal</span> from the world of <span class="h">Binah</span>. And through this Hashem redeems and guards us from the <span class="h">chitzonim</span>. For the <span class="h">Chashmal</span> guards the <span class="h">emunah</span>, as mentioned above. And this is: <span class="v">"You have redeemed me, Hashem, G-d of truth"</span> etc.</div>

<div class="p">And this is what we say in the <span class="h">berachah</span> of <span class="v">"HaShkivainu"</span> — which is a request for protection at night and during sleep — we request there: <span class="v">"spread over us the shelter of Your peace."</span> <span class="v">"The shelter of peace"</span> — this is the aspect of <span class="h">Ima d'masachchas al b'naha</span> [the Mother who shelters over her children], which is the aspect of <span class="h">Chashmal</span>, as explained in the Torah mentioned above. Therefore <span class="h">Chashmal</span> has the same <span class="h">gematria</span> as <span class="h">b'shalom</span> [in peace], as is brought — the aspect of <span class="v">"the shelter of peace."</span> For the essential guarding at night, during sleep, is through the aspect of <span class="h">Chashmal</span> — the aspect of the Mother who shelters over her children — through which the <span class="h">emunah</span> is guarded, as mentioned above. Through this, the mind and soul are renewed and repaired. And one merits to guard one's mind from evil thoughts and to think only holy thoughts — which is the essential <span class="h">teshuvah</span>, as mentioned above.</div>

<div class="p" style="text-align:center;font-weight:600;margin-top:2rem;">Amain v'Amain.</div>

<div class="end" style="margin-top:2rem;">
תם ונשלם שבח לאל בורא עולם<br>
Complete and finished — praise to G-d, Creator of the World.
</div>

